  "finding": "Each entry point wires its own copy of the weights it uses, even for identical graphs; one entry point wires one copy for text, image and 1-token steps. The compiled cache holds 2x the weights for one function and 3x for two (the VL-4B decoder's 22.48 GiB is 3 x 7.49 GiB). A separate embedding function wires its own copy of the tied table. Conversion keeps one copy however many programs share the weights; the export's peak comes from the eager module, the converter's copy, the save buffer and the cpu_only load (about 5x at its peak), not from per-program copies. One function retained more per request on the toy (1.9 MiB with a one-row image input, 2.9 MiB with aligned rows, against 0.9 MiB for two functions)."
